    "['mpls l2vc [parameter1] [parameter2] tunnel-policy [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 virtual-circuit-id 101', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the HUAWEI `mpls l2vc` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 virtual-circuit-id 101\\nset protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 tunnel-policy policy1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses separate commands for L2VC parameters\\n2. The interface name follows Juniper's naming convention (ge-2/0/0.1 instead of GigabitEthernet2/0/0.1)\\n3. The tunnel policy reference is maintained but would need corresponding tunnel policy configuration in Juniper\\n4. The QoS parameters (CIR/PIR) from the original configuration would need separate QoS configuration in Juniper\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 virtual-circuit-id 101'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 tunnel-policy policy1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the HUAWEI `mpls l2vc` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 virtual-circuit-id 101\\nset protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 tunnel-policy policy1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses separate commands for L2VC parameters\\n2. The interface name follows Juniper's naming convention (ge-2/0/0.1 instead of GigabitEthernet2/0/0.1)\\n3. The tunnel policy reference is maintained but would need corresponding tunnel policy configuration in Juniper\\n4. The QoS parameters (CIR/PIR) from the original configuration would need separate QoS configuration in Juniper\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols l2circuit neighbor 1.1.1.9 interface ge-2/0/0.1 tunnel-policy policy1'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls l2vpn qos cir [parameter1] pir [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [0, 4], 'trans_command': 'unit [parameter2] family [parameter3] address [parameter4] vrrp-group [parameter5] priority [parameter6]', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [1, 4], 'trans_command': 'unit [parameter2] family [parameter3] address [parameter4] vrrp-group [parameter5] priority [parameter6]', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['ip address unnumbered interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, the `unnumbered-address` parameter is used to assign an IP address from another interface (similar to HUAWEI's `unnumbered interface` command)\\n- `lo0.0` refers to the Loopback1 interface (Juniper typically uses lo0 for loopback)\\n- The command is applied under the interface's family inet configuration\\n- The syntax follows Juniper's hierarchical configuration structure\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol mpls te', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls interface all\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nIn Juniper, enabling MPLS TE (Traffic Engineering) is done by configuring MPLS protocols on interfaces. The command `tunnel-protocol mpls te` in Huawei specifies that the tunnel uses MPLS TE, which in Juniper is accomplished by enabling MPLS on interfaces (though Juniper handles TE capabilities differently as part of its MPLS protocol configuration). \\n\\nNote: The exact Juniper equivalent depends on the broader context, but this is the fundamental TE enabling command. A more complete TE configuration in Juniper would require additional TE-specific parameters.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface all'}]]": 1,
